*(
    title: Complex Numbers as Vectors
    description: Reading addition as displacement, subtraction as the arrow between two points, and multiplication by i as a quarter turn.
    tags: complex numbers, vectors, argand plane, rotation, geometric proof, extension 2
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    A complex number has already been drawn as a point in the Argand plane. Drawing it instead as an arrow from the origin to that point costs nothing, because the arrow and the point carry the same two numbers — $3 + i$ is three across and one up whichever way it is drawn.

    definition{
        The arrow from the origin to the point $(a, b)$, used to stand for $z = a + ib$. Its horizontal component is $\operatorname{Re}(z)$, its vertical component is $\operatorname{Im}(z)$, and its length is $|z|$.
    }(
        term: Vector representation
    )

    What the arrow buys is that it can be moved. An arrow keeps its meaning when it is slid to a new tail, since the components do not change, and that is exactly the freedom addition needs.

    ===

    To add $1 + 2i$ to $3 + i$, slide the second arrow so that its tail sits on the tip of the first. Its components are still $1$ and $2$; only its starting point has moved.

    ===

    The sum $4 + 3i$ is the arrow from the origin to the new tip: three plus one across, one plus two up. Adding in the other order slides $3 + i$ onto the tip of $1 + 2i$ and arrives at the same place, so the two routes close a parallelogram whose diagonal is the sum.

    note{
        The detour is never shorter than the direct route, so $|z_1 + z_2| \le |z_1| + |z_2|$, with equality only when the two arrows point the same way. This is the triangle inequality, and *Inequalities and Proof* proves it from the algebra.
    }
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: 3; dy: 1; colour: orange; label: $z_1 = 3 + i$)
        vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: 1; dy: 2; colour: purple; label: $z_2 = 1 + 2i$)

        cue.draw{
            vector(x: 3; y: 1; dx: 1; dy: 2; colour: purple; dashed)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1s
        )

        cue.draw{
            vector(x: 1; y: 2; dx: 3; dy: 1; colour: orange; dashed)
            vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: 4; dy: 3; colour: teal; label: $z_1 + z_2 = 4 + 3i$)
        }(
            in: 2
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
        x domain: [-1, 6]
        y domain: [-1, 5]
    )
]

scene{
    # Subtraction is the arrow between two points

    Subtracting is adding the negative, and the negative of an arrow is the same arrow reversed. So $z_2 - z_1$ is the arrow that starts at the tip of $z_1$ and ends at the tip of $z_2$: from $3 + i$ to $1 + 2i$ is two back and one up, which is $-2 + i$.

    ===

    Slid back to the origin it is the ordinary arrow for $-2 + i$, and nothing about it has changed. What the slid version makes visible is that the *difference* of two complex numbers lives between their points, not at the origin.

    ===

    That gives two of the most-used facts in the whole area a picture. The modulus $|z_2 - z_1|$ is the length of the arrow between the points, so it is the distance from $z_1$ to $z_2$ — here $\sqrt{4 + 1} = \sqrt{5}$. The argument $\arg(z_2 - z_1)$ is the direction from $z_1$ to $z_2$.

    definition{
        The modulus of their difference, $|z_2 - z_1|$, equal to the length of the segment joining the two points in the Argand plane.
    }(
        term: Distance between two complex numbers
    )

    The midpoint of the segment is $\frac{z_1 + z_2}{2}$, half of the parallelogram's diagonal. And a condition placed on $|z - z_0|$ or $\arg(z - z_0)$ is a condition on the distance or direction from a fixed point $z_0$, which is what turns an equation into a curve.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        point(x: 3; y: 1; colour: orange; label: $z_1 = 3 + i$)
        point(x: 1; y: 2; colour: purple; label: $z_2 = 1 + 2i$)

        cue.draw{
            vector(x: 3; y: 1; dx: -2; dy: 1; colour: teal; label: $z_2 - z_1$)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1s
        )

        cue.draw{
            vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: -2; dy: 1; colour: teal; dashed; label: $-2 + i$)
        }(
            in: 2
            in duration: 1s
        )

        cue{
            text(label: $|z_2 - z_1| = \sqrt{5}$; x: 2.6; y: 2.1; colour: teal)
        }(
            in: 3
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
        x domain: [-3, 4]
        y domain: [-1, 3]
    )
]

scene{
    # Multiplying by $i$ is a quarter turn

    Take $z = 3 + i$ and multiply by $i$: $iz = 3i + i^2 = -1 + 3i$. The components have swapped places and one of them has changed sign, so $(a, b)$ has become $(-b, a)$.

    ===

    That is the rule for turning an arrow through a right angle anticlockwise. The length is unchanged, since $\sqrt{b^2 + a^2} = \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$, and the two arrows are perpendicular — the square at the origin marks it.

    definition{
        Multiplying a complex number by $i$ rotates its arrow a quarter turn anticlockwise about the origin, leaving its modulus alone. Multiplying by $-i$ turns it a quarter turn clockwise.
    }(
        term: Multiplication by $i$
    )

    ===

    Multiply again and the turn repeats: $i^2 z = -z$ points the opposite way, $i^3 z = -iz$ is a quarter turn short of a full circle, and $i^4 z = z$ is home. The four powers of $i$ are the four quarter turns, which is a better reason for $i^4 = 1$ than any amount of algebra.

    This is the operation plane vectors have no equivalent of. The dot product of two vectors returns a number and throws the direction away; multiplication of two complex numbers returns a complex number, and the argument of the product is the sum of the arguments. Multiplying by $i$, with argument $\frac{\pi}{2}$, is the cleanest case of that rule, and *Polar Form and de Moivre's Theorem* gives the general one: multiplying by $\operatorname{cis}\theta$ turns the plane through $\theta$.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: 3; dy: 1; colour: orange; label: $z = 3 + i$)

        cue.draw{
            vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: -1; dy: 3; colour: teal; label: $iz = -1 + 3i$)
            angle(at: (0, 0); from: (3, 1); to: (-1, 3); colour: teal)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1s
        )

        cue.draw{
            vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: -3; dy: -1; colour: grey; dashed; label: $i^2 z = -z$)
            vector(x: 0; y: 0; dx: 1; dy: -3; colour: grey; dashed; label: $i^3 z$)
        }(
            in: 2
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
        x domain: [-4, 4]
        y domain: [-4, 4]
    )
]

scene{
    ## A geometric proof: the diagonals of a rhombus

    Let $z_1$ and $z_2$ have the same modulus $r$, so the parallelogram they span has four equal sides — a rhombus. On the canvas $z_1 = 3 + i$ and $z_2 = 1 + 3i$, both of modulus $\sqrt{10}$. The diagonals are $z_1 + z_2$ from the origin and $z_1 - z_2$ across the middle, and the claim is that they are perpendicular.

    ===

    Two arrows are perpendicular when their quotient has argument $\pm\frac{\pi}{2}$, that is, when the quotient is purely imaginary. So write $w = \frac{z_1 + z_2}{z_1 - z_2}$ and aim to show $w + \bar{w} = 0$.

    The tool is that $z\bar{z} = |z|^2$, so a number of modulus $r$ has conjugate $\bar{z} = \frac{r^2}{z}$. Conjugating $w$ and substituting that in for both $\bar{z}_1$ and $\bar{z}_2$:

    equation{
        \bar{w} = \frac{\bar{z}_1 + \bar{z}_2}{\bar{z}_1 - \bar{z}_2} = \frac{r^2/z_1 + r^2/z_2}{r^2/z_1 - r^2/z_2} = \frac{z_2 + z_1}{z_2 - z_1} = -w
    }

    The last step multiplies top and bottom by $\frac{z_1 z_2}{r^2}$. So $\bar{w} = -w$, which forces $\operatorname{Re}(w) = 0$, and the diagonals are perpendicular. With the numbers on the canvas, $w = \frac{4 + 4i}{2 - 2i} = 2i$, exactly as promised.

    The proof never drew a triangle or named an angle. It turned a geometric claim into a statement about a quotient, and the algebra of conjugates did the rest.

    task{
        $A = 1 + i$ and $B = 4 + 2i$ are adjacent vertices of a square $ABCD$, labelled anticlockwise. Find $C$ and $D$.
    }[
        The side $B - A = 3 + i$ turned a quarter turn anticlockwise is $i(3 + i) = -1 + 3i$. Adding it to each end of the side gives $D = A + (-1 + 3i) = 4i$ and $C = B + (-1 + 3i) = 3 + 5i$.
    ](
        title: Your turn
    )

    Once a difference is an arrow, its length and direction can be constrained. Conditions of that kind cut out circles, rays and half-planes, which is the subject of *Curves and Regions in the Complex Plane*.

    internote(ref: curves-and-regions.chalk)
}[
    canvas.graph{
        point(x: 0; y: 0; colour: grey; label: $O$)
        polygon(x: 0, 3, 4, 1; y: 0, 1, 4, 3; colour: teal; !fill)
        point(x: 3; y: 1; colour: orange; label: $z_1$)
        point(x: 1; y: 3; colour: purple; label: $z_2$)

        cue.draw{
            segment(start: (0, 0); end: (4, 4); colour: orange; label: $z_1 + z_2$)
            segment(start: (1, 3); end: (3, 1); colour: purple; label: $z_1 - z_2$)
            angle(at: (2, 2); from: (4, 4); to: (3, 1); colour: grey)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1.2s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
        x domain: [-1, 5]
        y domain: [-1, 5]
    )
]
